1
00:00:05,810 --> 00:00:10,230
This is a role-based candidate
screening system, built with a Next.js

2
00:00:10,270 --> 00:00:15,570
front end, a FastAPI back end, and a
database that stores every session.

3
00:00:16,190 --> 00:00:20,550
The idea is simple: instead of a fixed
list of questions, the interview

4
00:00:20,590 --> 00:00:24,310
is built from the candidate's own
resume and the role they applied for.

5
00:00:24,910 --> 00:00:29,170
I enter the candidate's name,
pick the role — here, AI/ML

6
00:00:29,210 --> 00:00:32,490
Backend Developer Intern —
and load a sample resume.

7
00:00:32,530 --> 00:00:38,190
The moment the resume text arrives, the
system parses it and shows what it found:

8
00:00:38,650 --> 00:00:41,610
skills, technologies, and the domains the

9
00:00:41,650 --> 00:00:44,590
candidate has worked in,
shown as these chips.

10
00:00:46,170 --> 00:00:50,630
Resumes can also be uploaded
as a PDF — this is a second

11
00:00:50,670 --> 00:00:54,670
sample candidate, and her file
is parsed exactly the same way.

12
00:00:54,710 --> 00:01:00,770
Switching back to Rohan and starting the
session, the system combines his parsed

13
00:01:00,810 --> 00:01:05,670
profile with the role's key skills and
builds a retrieval query from them.

14
00:01:06,270 --> 00:01:11,150
It then searches its knowledge base — short
notes on machine learning and back-end

15
00:01:11,190 --> 00:01:16,690
topics — and every question it asks is
generated from a passage it retrieved.

16
00:01:16,730 --> 00:01:21,250
The exact source is shown under
the question, together with the

17
00:01:21,290 --> 00:01:25,210
passage itself, so the grounding
can be checked on the spot.

18
00:01:26,550 --> 00:01:29,210
The candidate answers in his own words.

19
00:01:29,670 --> 00:01:32,610
Because this answer is
detailed and uses the concepts

20
00:01:32,650 --> 00:01:35,730
from the source passage,
the system follows up

21
00:01:35,770 --> 00:01:38,770
on the same topic, with
a second question drawn

22
00:01:38,810 --> 00:01:41,870
from the same retrieved
note, instead of moving on.

23
00:01:41,910 --> 00:01:46,470
A short or thin answer simply
moves to the next topic.

24
00:01:47,290 --> 00:01:50,170
Either way, every question and every answer

25
00:01:50,210 --> 00:01:52,430
is written to the database as the session

26
00:01:52,470 --> 00:01:58,310
goes, so the complete interview can be
reviewed afterwards, exactly as it happened.

27
00:01:59,610 --> 00:02:03,730
The remaining questions follow the
same pattern, one topic at a time.

28
00:02:03,770 --> 00:02:08,530
Database design comes next, including
how to stop two users booking

29
00:02:08,570 --> 00:02:12,370
the same appointment slot — a
problem from his own clinic project.

30
00:02:13,090 --> 00:02:18,430
Then REST API design, how a retrieval-augmented
generation pipeline works end to

31
00:02:18,470 --> 00:02:24,790
end, React and Next.js rendering, and Naive
Bayes, from the machine learning notes.

32
00:02:24,830 --> 00:02:29,990
Six topics in total, each one chosen
because the resume and the role pointed

33
00:02:30,030 --> 00:02:34,030
to it, and each question traceable
to the passage it was grounded in.

34
00:02:35,230 --> 00:02:37,990
When the interview ends, the system writes

35
00:02:38,030 --> 00:02:40,230
a structured summary of the whole session.

36
00:02:40,850 --> 00:02:44,230
There is an overall score,
the average grounding across

37
00:02:44,270 --> 00:02:48,150
all answers, and a table that
breaks the session down by

38
00:02:48,190 --> 00:02:52,450
topic: how many questions were
asked, how well the answers

39
00:02:52,490 --> 00:02:56,190
used the reference concepts,
and how detailed they were.

40
00:02:56,950 --> 00:03:00,510
It also lists plain-language
insights — the strongest

41
00:03:00,550 --> 00:03:03,970
topic, the resume skills
the session never got to

42
00:03:04,010 --> 00:03:07,710
probe, and how much of the
plan was covered — along

43
00:03:07,750 --> 00:03:10,970
with the exact retrieval
query it used at the start.

44
00:03:12,410 --> 00:03:15,010
Under the hood, everything is inspectable.

45
00:03:15,490 --> 00:03:19,710
The system's OpenAPI specification
lists every endpoint —

46
00:03:19,750 --> 00:03:23,510
resume parsing, sessions,
answers, and summaries — and the

47
00:03:23,550 --> 00:03:27,110
knowledge base itself is exposed
through the API, so anyone

48
00:03:27,150 --> 00:03:30,190
can see exactly which notes
the questions came from.

49
00:03:30,910 --> 00:03:35,050
In this demo the system runs in
its fully offline mode, so each

50
00:03:35,090 --> 00:03:38,590
question can be traced back to a
retrieved note, word for word.

51
00:03:38,630 --> 00:03:40,190
Thank you for watching.
